    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Enjoy your meal.' Russian leaves out 'am', 'is', or 'are' in the present tense, linking 'pleasant' straight to 'appetite'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Что у тебя с поваром?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What's going on with the chef?' Notice how 'what' sets up the clause and leads naturally into 'as a cook'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Поговорим после работы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's talk after work.' Kristina puts 'we'll have a talk' up front so the listener immediately catches the tone, followed by 'of work'. And Sasha jumps in.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "А что случилось?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And what happened?' Sasha links 'and' directly with 'what' to express the thought clearly.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Я иду к врачу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I am going to the doctor.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'I' with 'the doctor'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ты беременна?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you pregnant?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'pregnant'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "С ума сошла?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Are you crazy?' Notice how the verb meaning 'went off' takes the feminine past-tense ending '-a' here.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Это просто сыпь.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'It's just a rash.' Kristina combines 'this' with 'rash' in a natural Russian turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Хорошо, я поняла.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Okay, I got it.' This everyday Russian sentence pairs 'good' with 'understood'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Закажи мне роллы.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Order me some rolls.' Kristina uses the imperative command form of the verb meaning 'order'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "И зелёный чай?",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'And green tea?' Russian forms this yes-or-no question without a question word, relying on rising pitch on 'tea'. Then Kristina responds.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "kristina",
      "t": "Да, пожалуйста.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
